---
description: Automatic post-merge worktree cleanup and sandbox-safe Git ownership
alwaysApply: true
---

# Worktree lifecycle

- After implementation and merge, the primary agent MUST run
  `scripts/cleanup-worktrees.sh --tmp` without requesting permission. Pass the base branch
  explicitly when it is not `staging`. Cleanup is required before declaring completion.
- Only delete a worktree if its branch passes `git merge-base --is-ancestor` against the
  base and it has no tracked or untracked changes. Never delete the main checkout,
  unmerged branches, locked worktrees, or detached worktrees. List preserved paths and reasons.
- Never bypass a failed safety check, lock, or Git removal with raw filesystem deletion.
- Sandbox workers never write `.git`: no commit, merge, fetch, worktree add/remove/prune,
  branch mutations, or index writes. Workers edit files and report results; the primary
  agent performs Git mutations in a runtime with the required access.
- Check primary runtime permissions first. Claude allow rules do not grant Codex filesystem
  access. If Git metadata is read-only, report the blocked step without repeated approvals
  or pretending cleanup succeeded.
- Create worktrees only in `../<repo>-wt/<name>`. Never sweep neighboring projects or windows.
- Active sessions must lock their worktrees through the primary agent before workers start
  and unlock only after workers finish. Never unlock another session's worktree. A clean,
  merged branch alone cannot identify a running session.
- Never leave repository copies, archives, logs, or dependency caches in shared temporary
  folders. Use the session scratchpad and remove disposable artifacts after completion.
- `--tmp` only considers top-level `pandatrack-*` artifacts in `$TMPDIR` and `/private/tmp`.
  It preserves registered checkouts and directories containing Git metadata. Repository
  copies require a separate safety review; never bypass the merge/clean requirements.
- Permanent deletion is enabled by default; `--dry-run` is available for inspection.
  Poll long-running shell sessions to completion and keep the owner informed. The script
  waits with progress; do not impose a two-minute kill timeout or abandon deletion.
- Report the before/after `df` delta, preserved paths, and failures.
